1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,479
Assalamu Alaikum, welcome to LifeLink Marriage
Academy brought to you by Educating

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:07,380
Excellence Group.

3
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:12,499
We are delighted that you have taken this important
first step towards preparing for

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,760
one of the most significant
relationships of your life.

5
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:21,199
Whether you are considering marriage for the
first time, have previously been

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:26,859
married or are now exploring the possibility of
remarriage, this programme has been

7
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:28,420
developed to support you.

8
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:33,580
At LifeLink we believe that finding the right
life partner is important,

9
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:39,580
however we also believe that becoming the right
life partner is equally important.

10
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:45,639
Marriage is not simply about finding someone who
meets a list of expectations, it is

11
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,660
about developing the knowledge,
emotional maturity,

12
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:54,080
understanding and character needed to build
a successful relationship.

13
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:58,960
In Islam, marriage, or nikah, is a
sacred covenant.

14
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:04,300
It is a commitment founded upon faith,
compassion, responsibility,

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,860
trust and mutual respect.

16
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:13,780
It is not simply about finding someone you love
or planning a beautiful wedding.

17
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:19,460
A wedding may last a day, but the responsibilities
of marriage can last a lifetime.

18
00:01:20,190 --> 00:01:23,900
This is why we have developed the
LifeLink Marriage Academy.

19
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:30,039
Across our 18 compulsory modules, we will explore
the foundations of a successful

20
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:34,639
marriage, from choosing a compatible partner
and understanding Islamic

21
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,660
responsibilities to communication,

22
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,120
managing conflict, finances, family
relationships and maintaining love.

23
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:47,679
Our aim is not simply to help you prepare for
marriage, but to equip you with the

24
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,480
tools to build a healthy, respectful and
lasting relationship.

25
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:57,299
In this first module, we will explore the sacred
purpose of marriage and what it

26
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:02,040
means to enter this partnership with the right
intentions and expectations.

27
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,499
As you listen, I encourage you to reflect not
only on the qualities you want in a

28
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:12,880
future spouse, but also on the qualities you
are bringing into this marriage.

29
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,440
Let us begin, Smillah.

30
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:21,519
Before we explore the foundations of marriage, I
would like to explain what we will

31
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,180
be learning throughout this module.

32
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:27,940
Our journey begins with understanding the
divine purpose of marriage.

33
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:34,039
We will look at why marriage holds such an
important position in Islam and how it

34
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:37,300
can contribute to our spiritual and
personal development.

35
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:41,720
We will then explore what I call the
marital ecosystem.

36
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:45,100
Marriage does not exist in isolation.

37
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:51,360
It affects our emotional well -being, family
relationships, financial circumstances,

38
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,200
spiritual development and wider
responsibilities.

39
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:01,980
Next, we will consider the concept of
partnership and a manner of sacred trust.

40
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:06,560
What does it actually mean to share your
life with another individual?

41
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:12,500
How do we balance our own needs and ambitions
with the needs of someone else?

42
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:17,000
We will also examine the qualities of a
successful marriage.

43
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:22,399
Often when people are searching for a spouse, they
focus on external qualities such

44
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:28,240
as appearance, education, employment, family
background or financial stability.

45
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:33,859
Although some of these considerations may be
important, we must also look

46
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:39,840
at the emotional maturity and the
ability to communicate.

47
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,720
We will then explore realistic
expectations.

48
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:49,559
Many relationships experience difficulties because
individuals enter marriage with

49
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:51,700
assumptions that have never
been discussed.

50
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:56,220
Finally, we will complete what we
call the mirror check.

51
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:02,240
This is an opportunity to look inward and reflect
on our own readiness for marriage.

52
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:06,940
Throughout this module, remember that the
aim is not perfection.

53
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:12,259
None of us are perfect and marriage does not
require us to become perfect

54
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:13,060
individuals.

55
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:18,000
However, it does require us to recognise
our responsibilities,

56
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:23,180
understand our strengths and identify areas
in which we need to grow.

57
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:31,199
Beautiful descriptions of marriage in the Quran
appears in Surah Arghum, verse

58
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,100
21.

59
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:38,639
Allah tells us that amongst his signs is that he
created spouses so that we may find

60
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:43,380
tranquility in them and that he places
affection and mercy between them.

61
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:48,300
This verse introduces three essential
pillars of marriage.

62
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:52,180
The first is sakina, which
means tranquility.

63
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:55,980
Think about what tranquility means
in a relationship.

64
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:01,239
It means feeling emotionally secure, knowing
that you can express your thoughts

65
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:06,879
without fear or humiliation and trusting that
your spouse will treat you with

66
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,300
dignity and respect.

67
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:12,440
A peaceful marriage does not mean that
disagreements never happen.

68
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:17,799
It means that even when difficulties arise,
both individuals are committed to

69
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:20,840
resolving them without causing
unnecessary harm.

70
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:24,980
The second pillar is mawuddha
or affection.

71
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:28,880
Affection is more than simply telling
someone that you love them.

72
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:32,400
It is demonstrated through
everyday actions.

73
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:36,760
A kind word, a thoughtful gesture,
showing appreciation,

74
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:42,600
spending meaningful time together or offering
support after a difficult day.

75
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,340
These small acts strengthen
emotional connection.

76
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:51,480
The third pillar is rahmah, meaning mercy.

77
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:55,640
Mercy becomes particularly important
when life is challenging.

78
00:05:56,660 --> 00:06:01,800
There will be times when one spouse is tired,
unwell, worried or struggling.

79
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:07,200
During these moments compassion and understanding
can make an enormous difference.

80
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:12,840
Marriage requires us to recognise that our
spouse is human with strengths,

81
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:15,900
weaknesses, emotions and vulnerabilities.

82
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,900
Now consider these three
qualities together.

83
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:23,300
Tranquility provides emotional security.

84
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:26,180
If affection builds connection,

85
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,380
mercy helps sustain the relationship
during difficulty.

86
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:32,900
Now reflect for a moment.

87
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:39,019
When you think about your future marriage, which
of these three qualities do you

88
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:41,459
believe you already demonstrate well?

89
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:44,500
And which would you like to
develop further?

90
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:53,379
Marriage is often described as a relationship
between two people, but its influence

91
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:56,140
extends into many different areas of life.

92
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:01,160
On this slide we can see the different
dimensions of a healthy marriage.

93
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:04,940
At the centre is lawful
intimacy and faith.

94
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:10,799
Marriage provides an Islamic framework for
physical affection and intimacy, but it

95
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:16,359
also provides opportunities for companionship,
emotional connection and spiritual

96
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:17,160
growth.

97
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:20,180
Let us begin with companionship
and belonging.

98
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:24,100
Human beings naturally seek connection.

99
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:29,979
Having someone to share experiences with, celebrate
achievements with and turning to

100
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:33,940
during difficult times can be a valuable
part of married life.

101
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:38,660
Another important dimension is emotional
and practical support.

102
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,340
For example, if one spouse
experiences illness,

103
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:49,519
loses their employment or faces a family
difficulty, the other spouse may be able to

104
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:51,540
provide reassurance and assistance.

105
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:56,800
Marriage also provides opportunities to
strengthen faith and character.

106
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:01,760
Living closely with another person can
teach us patience, generosity,

107
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:05,400
gratitude, compromise and self
-reflection.

108
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:09,080
Another dimension is shared
responsibility.

109
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:15,420
A couple must make decisions about finances,
accommodation, family relationships,

110
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:18,420
household responsibilities and
their future.

111
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:23,460
These responsibilities require
communication and cooperation.

112
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,779
For couples who choose to have children, marriage
may also provide a foundation for

113
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:29,660
raising a family.

114
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:35,559
However, it is important to recognise that not
every couple will have children, and

115
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:39,220
a marriage does not lose its value or
purpose because of this.

116
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:43,200
I want to emphasise one
particularly important point.

117
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:47,660
The benefit of marriage are
opportunities, not guarantees.

118
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:53,459
Simply getting married does not automatically
create happiness, emotional security

119
00:08:53,460 --> 00:08:55,460
or a healthy relationship.

120
00:08:56,540 --> 00:09:00,180
Both individuals must contribute towards
building those qualities.

121
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:05,400
A successful marriage requires continuing
effort from both spouses.

122
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:11,999
This slide introduces one of the most important
lessons in our entire marriage

123
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:12,800
academy.

124
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:18,159
Prophet Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him,
thought that the best amongst us

125
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,420
are those who are best to their wives.

126
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:25,979
This teaching reminds us that good character
must extend beyond our public

127
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:26,780
behaviour.

128
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:31,540
Sometimes individuals are highly respected
within their communities.

129
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:35,880
They may be known for their religious
knowledge, professional achievements,

130
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:37,880
generosity or public service.

131
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:42,939
However, the way someone behaves in public does
not always reflect the way they

132
00:09:42,940 --> 00:09:43,820
behave at home.

133
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:48,880
Our closest relationships often reveal
our true character.

134
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:54,440
It is relatively easy to show patience and
kindness when everything is going well.

135
00:09:54,940 --> 00:10:00,060
The greater challenge is maintaining those
qualities when we are tired, frustrated,

136
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:02,220
disappointed or under pressure.

137
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:04,400
Consider this example.

138
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:09,299
Someone may speak politely to colleagues
throughout the working day but return home

139
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:11,240
and speak harshly to their spouse.

140
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:16,379
They may show respect to friends and relatives but
dismiss their spouse's feelings

141
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:17,180
or opinions.

142
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:21,920
This inconsistency is something we
should all reflect upon.

143
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:26,160
Islamic character is not simply
about what others see.

144
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:31,960
It is about integrity, sincerity and how we treat
people when there is no audience.

145
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:39,039
As you prepare for marriage, I encourage you to
ask yourself, am I as respectful

146
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:43,500
towards those closest to me as I am
towards people outside my home?

147
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:46,560
How do I respond when I am frustrated?

148
00:10:47,660 --> 00:10:49,379
Do I apologise when I am wrong?

149
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:54,600
Can I acknowledge another person's feelings
even when I disagree with them?

150
00:10:55,780 --> 00:11:00,599
These are important questions because the quality
of our private character will have

151
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,520
a significant influence on the
quality of our marriage.

152
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:08,960
Remember, religious practice and good character
should reinforce one another.

153
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:16,260
Marriage today takes place within a world
that is constantly changing.

154
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:20,879
Although the Islamic principles that guide
marriage remain relevant, the

155
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:25,020
circumstances in which couples live have
changed significantly.

156
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:28,200
Let us consider some of these challenges.

157
00:11:29,380 --> 00:11:30,180
Firstly,

158
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:31,500
financial realities.

159
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:37,439
Many couples are dealing with rising living
costs, housing expenses and the

160
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:40,220
pressures of maintaining
financial stability.

161
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:45,839
These pressures can affect when individuals feel
ready to marry, where they choose

162
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:48,460
to live and how they organise their
responsibilities.

163
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:53,300
Secondly, we have the influence of
technology in social media.

164
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,500
We are constantly exposed to images of
seemingly perfect relationships.

165
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:04,299
We see expensive weddings, romantic holidays,
beautiful homes and couples presenting

166
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:07,120
an idealised version of their lives.

167
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:11,000
But what we see online is rarely the
complete picture.

168
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:17,399
We do not necessarily see the disagreements,
sacrifices, compromises or challenges

169
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:20,180
that every relationship may experience.

170
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:25,779
Comparing our own relationship with these
carefully selected moments can create

171
00:12:25,780 --> 00:12:29,400
dissatisfaction and unrealistic
expectations.

172
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:34,740
Thirdly, we have changing expectations around
roles and responsibilities.

173
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:40,380
For example, both spouses may be pursuing
careers or further education.

174
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:46,099
Couples may need to discuss how they will
balance work, family life, household

175
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:48,780
responsibilities and their
individual ambitions.

176
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:52,560
Cultural differences can also
create challenges.

177
00:12:53,420 --> 00:12:57,739
Even when two individuals share the same
faith, their family traditions and

178
00:12:57,740 --> 00:12:59,680
expectations may be very different.

179
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:05,719
The important question is not whether these
differences exist but how they are

180
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:06,520
approached.

181
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:13,219
Islamic principles of justice, compassion,
responsibility and consultation provide a

182
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:15,980
foundation for navigating these
circumstances.

183
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:22,079
The goal is not to recreate someone else's
marriage, it is to develop a relationship

184
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:27,039
that is faithful to Islamic principles while
being realistic about your own

185
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:27,920
circumstances.

186
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:37,459
One of the challenges faced by many Muslim couples
is distinguishing between Islamic

187
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:40,180
teachings and cultural expectations.

188
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:43,880
Culture can be a beautiful part
of our identity.

189
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:48,939
It gives us traditions, languages, customs and
connections with our families and

190
00:13:48,940 --> 00:13:49,740
communities.

191
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:55,759
However, difficulties can arise when cultural
expectations are presented as though

192
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:57,260
they are religious obligations.

193
00:13:58,220 --> 00:14:00,020
Let us look at some examples.

194
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,499
In certain communities, there may be significant
pressure to organize an expensive

195
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:06,300
wedding.

196
00:14:06,880 --> 00:14:11,199
Families may feel that a wedding must be lavish
to maintain their reputation or

197
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,800
demonstrate social standing.

198
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:18,259
However, the size or cost of a wedding is not
a measure of the quality of a

199
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:19,060
marriage.

200
00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:23,939
A couple may begin married life with a very simple
celebration and go on to build a

201
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:26,320
loving and successful relationship.

202
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:32,620
Another example is the emphasis placed on social
status and family reputation.

203
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:39,060
Sometimes individuals are encouraged to choose a
spouse primarily because of wealth,

204
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,000
professional standing or family
connections.

205
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:47,699
Although practical circumstances are worth
considering, these should not overshadow

206
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:50,200
character, faith and compatibility.

207
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,940
We must also consider household
responsibilities.

208
00:14:55,820 --> 00:14:59,979
Different cultures may have different
expectations about what husbands and wives

209
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:01,200
should do within the home.

210
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:07,219
It is important to understand Islamic rights
and responsibilities that's also

211
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:12,120
discussing how a couple will organize their daily
lives fairly and respectfully.

212
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:18,019
The key message here is that cultural traditions
should not be used to justify

213
00:15:18,020 --> 00:15:21,740
injustice, humiliation or coercion.

214
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:26,040
When preparing for marriage, ask
yourself these questions.

215
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:30,340
Which of my expectations come from
Islamic teachings?

216
00:15:31,530 --> 00:15:34,840
Which come from my family or
cultural background?

217
00:15:35,540 --> 00:15:38,620
And which are simply my
personal preferences?

218
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:45,359
Understanding these differences can prevent
misunderstandings and unnecessary

219
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:46,520
conflict.

220
00:15:48,250 --> 00:15:52,660
This brings together two individuals who may
have very different personalities,

221
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:54,800
experiences and perspectives.

222
00:15:55,680 --> 00:15:59,779
Even when two people share the same religious
values, they will not necessarily

223
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:00,820
think alike.

224
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:05,880
One person may be naturally outgoing, whilst
the other is more reserved.

225
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:10,160
One may prefer careful planning, whilst the
other is more spontaneous.

226
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,159
One may express affection through words, whilst
the other demonstrates care through

227
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:16,320
practical actions.

228
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:21,300
These differences do not automatically mean
that two people are incompatible.

229
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:26,920
What matters is how they understand, respect and
respond to those differences.

230
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:32,400
A healthy marriage is built on partnership
rather than competition.

231
00:16:33,260 --> 00:16:36,060
Let us consider the four elements
shown on this slide.

232
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:39,200
The first is respectful consultation.

233
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:44,359
Important decisions should involve meaningful
discussion, particularly when these

234
00:16:44,360 --> 00:16:46,080
decisions affect both spouses.

235
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:49,180
The second is shared responsibilities.

236
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:54,419
Marriage involves practical commitments and
couples need to understand how these

237
00:16:54,420 --> 00:16:56,159
responsibilities will be managed.

238
00:16:56,160 --> 00:17:01,739
This does not necessarily mean dividing every
task equally, it means developing

239
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:05,060
arrangements that are fair,
understood and agreed upon.

240
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:07,900
The third is mutual support.

241
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:13,139
A successful partnership involves supporting one
another's personal, professional

242
00:17:13,140 --> 00:17:14,960
and spiritual development.

243
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:20,819
For example, if one spouse wishes to continue
their education, a supportive partner

244
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:25,779
will consider how that ambition can be
accommodated alongside their shared

245
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:26,580
responsibilities.

246
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:31,200
The fourth is honouring individuality.

247
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:35,180
Being married does not mean
losing your identity.

248
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:42,119
Both spouses remain individuals with their own
personalities, interests, strengths

249
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:43,200
and aspirations.

250
00:17:44,660 --> 00:17:48,879
Marriage should provide opportunities for growth
rather than requiring one person to

251
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:51,220
disappear into their
expectations of another.

252
00:17:52,100 --> 00:17:56,700
Ultimately, a healthy partnership is not
about asking who has more power.

253
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:02,459
It's about asking how can we work together to
build a relationship in which both of

254
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:05,680
us are respected, supported and valued.

255
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:12,000
Now come to a particularly important
concept, a manna.

256
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:15,020
A manna means trust or responsibility.

257
00:18:16,080 --> 00:18:20,979
In marriage, this means recognizing that our
spouse is someone whose dignity, well

258
00:18:20,980 --> 00:18:23,280
-being and rights must be respected.

259
00:18:24,240 --> 00:18:30,640
Allah tells us in Surah An -Nisa verse 19 to
live with our spouses in kindness.

260
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:36,739
This principle should influence how we speak,
behave and make decisions within

261
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:37,540
marriage.

262
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:43,619
A healthy understanding of a manna involves
recognizing that both spouses have

263
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:45,660
responsibilities towards one another.

264
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:50,540
It means respecting each other's feelings,
boundaries and dignity.

265
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:55,780
It also means approaching disagreements with
compassion rather than intimidation.

266
00:18:56,780 --> 00:19:02,020
However, it is important to distinguish
between responsibility and control.

267
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:04,600
Marriage is not ownership.

268
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:10,100
Neither spouse has the right to use marriage as an
excuse for humiliation, threats,

269
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:12,000
violence or coercion.

270
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:16,899
For example, preventing the spouse from
maintaining appropriate relationships with

271
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:22,020
family and friends as a means of isolating them is
not a healthy expression of care.

272
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:24,299
Similarly,

273
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:28,979
monitoring someone's every movement, using
money to control their behavior or

274
00:19:28,980 --> 00:19:33,280
threatening them to obtain compliance can be
signs of coercive control.

275
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:40,179
Sometimes harmful behaviors are wrongfully
justified through cultural expectations

276
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:42,380
or selective religious arguments.

277
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:47,480
We must be very clear that abuse is not a
demonstration of Islamic responsibility.

278
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,440
Let us also consider commitment.

279
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:56,200
Marriage requires patience, effort and the
willingness to work through difficulties.

280
00:19:57,220 --> 00:20:01,779
However, commitment never means that someone
must tolerate abuse or remain in

281
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:02,580
danger.

282
00:20:03,860 --> 00:20:08,440
A healthy relationship should protect the dignity
and safety of both individuals.

283
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:13,879
As you prepare for marriage, remember that
trust is built through consistent

284
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:14,680
behavior.

285
00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:19,600
It develops when people feel respected,
listened to and emotionally safe.

286
00:20:20,400 --> 00:20:26,139
Amana is about protecting the trust placed in us,
not using that trust to exercise

287
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:27,980
power over another person.

288
00:20:29,580 --> 00:20:34,459
We have now explored the spiritual foundations
of marriage, the importance of

289
00:20:34,460 --> 00:20:36,400
partnership and the concept of trust.

290
00:20:36,920 --> 00:20:41,319
Let us bring these ideas together by considering
the qualities that contribute to a

291
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:42,300
successful marriage.

292
00:20:43,240 --> 00:20:46,680
This slide organizes those qualities
into three areas.

293
00:20:47,060 --> 00:20:48,820
The first is the core foundation.

294
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:52,120
This includes faith, respect and trust.

295
00:20:53,100 --> 00:20:55,820
Faith provides a moral and
spiritual foundation.

296
00:20:56,540 --> 00:21:01,500
Respect means recognizing your spouse's dignity,
opinions, needs and feelings.

297
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:07,100
Trust develops when we are honest, reliable
and consistent in our behavior.

298
00:21:08,420 --> 00:21:10,480
The second area is action.

299
00:21:11,680 --> 00:21:15,860
A successful marriage is not built on
good intentions alone.

300
00:21:16,280 --> 00:21:19,320
Our values must be reflected in our
everyday behavior.

301
00:21:20,360 --> 00:21:22,300
Communication is particularly important.

302
00:21:23,020 --> 00:21:27,179
Being able to listen carefully, express our
feelings respectfully and discuss

303
00:21:27,180 --> 00:21:30,720
difficult subjects helps prevent
misunderstandings.

304
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:34,040
Responsibility is equally important.

305
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:39,559
Both spouses must understand their commitments
and take accountability for their

306
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:40,360
actions.

307
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:45,179
Cooperation allows couples to work together rather
than treating every disagreement

308
00:21:45,180 --> 00:21:46,220
as a competition.

309
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:49,640
The third area is grace and repair.

310
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:55,500
This is especially important because no
marriage will be free from mistakes.

311
00:21:56,540 --> 00:22:00,980
Compassion allows us to recognize when our spouse
may be experiencing difficulty.

312
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:06,380
Patience helps us manage frustration without
reacting in harmful ways.

313
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:11,420
Appreciation reminds us to recognize the
effort our spouse makes.

314
00:22:12,260 --> 00:22:16,580
Forgiveness, where appropriate, allows couples to
address mistakes and move forward.

315
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:21,639
However, forgiveness should not be confused
with ignoring repeated harmful

316
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:22,440
behaviors.

317
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:27,160
Genuine repair requires accountability
and meaningful change.

318
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:30,620
Consider a couple who experiences
a disagreement.

319
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:33,900
One spouse makes an insensitive comment.

320
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:40,099
A healthy response would involve recognizing the
impact of that comment, apologizing

321
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:44,140
sincerely, and making an effort not to
repeat that behavior.

322
00:22:45,140 --> 00:22:46,960
This is how relationships grow.

323
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:50,140
Not through perfection, but
through responsibility,

324
00:22:50,860 --> 00:22:53,440
reflection, and consistent effort.

325
00:22:54,700 --> 00:22:58,060
It's easy to talk about kindness,
respect, and responsibility,

326
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:03,000
but the real challenge is demonstrating these
qualities in everyday life.

327
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:06,880
Let us explore three practical scenarios.

328
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:09,980
Our first scenario is about appreciation.

329
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,220
Imagine that one spouse has returned home
after a demanding day.

330
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:18,840
They've been managing several
responsibilities and are feeling exhausted.

331
00:23:20,340 --> 00:23:22,220
The other spouse notices this.

332
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:27,999
Rather than assuming that their efforts are simply
expected, they acknowledge what

333
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:29,900
has been done and offer support.

334
00:23:30,660 --> 00:23:34,579
A simple statement such as I can see you've
had a difficult day, queue for

335
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:38,160
everything you've done, can make someone
feel recognized and valued.

336
00:23:39,480 --> 00:23:43,300
Cretiation does not always require expensive
gifts or elaborate gestures.

337
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:48,060
Often it is demonstrated through small,
consistent acts of kindness.

338
00:23:49,260 --> 00:23:51,720
Our second scenario involves disagreement.

339
00:23:53,380 --> 00:23:57,519
Imagine that a couple have different opinions
about a significant financial

340
00:23:57,520 --> 00:23:58,320
decision.

341
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:01,640
One spouse wants to make a major purchase.

342
00:24:02,240 --> 00:24:04,340
What's the other is concerned
about affordability.

343
00:24:05,620 --> 00:24:11,219
An unhealthy response would be to dismiss the
other person's concerns or make the

344
00:24:11,220 --> 00:24:12,700
decision without consultation.

345
00:24:13,640 --> 00:24:18,499
A healthy response would be to listen carefully,
explain their perspectives, and

346
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:19,980
work towards an agreement.

347
00:24:20,840 --> 00:24:23,200
The aim should not be to win the argument.

348
00:24:23,720 --> 00:24:28,760
It should be to reach a decision that both
individuals understand and can support.

349
00:24:29,640 --> 00:24:32,140
Our third scenario is about
accountability.

350
00:24:33,320 --> 00:24:37,000
Imagine that one spouse makes a hurtful
comment during a disagreement.

351
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:43,059
Instead of blaming the other person or minimizing
what happened, they acknowledge

352
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:43,860
the mistake.

353
00:24:44,340 --> 00:24:49,080
They apologize sincerely and consider how they
can respond differently in future.

354
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:52,320
This is emotional maturity in action.

355
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:57,599
These examples remind us that successful
relationships are built through everyday

356
00:24:57,600 --> 00:24:58,400
choices

357
00:24:59,060 --> 00:25:02,500
I want you to reflect on the
following questions.

358
00:25:03,220 --> 00:25:04,780
Which of these three qualities,

359
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:11,599
appreciation, respectful, disagreement, or
accountability do you think you must need

360
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:12,400
to strengthen?

361
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:20,620
We now turn to one of the most common causes of
disappointment in relationships,

362
00:25:21,260 --> 00:25:22,980
unrealistic expectations.

363
00:25:23,800 --> 00:25:27,800
Many people enter marriage with ideas about what
married life should look like.

364
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:32,859
These expectations may come from our
upbringing, cultural experiences, family

365
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:35,220
relationships, films, or social media.

366
00:25:36,180 --> 00:25:38,720
Some expectations are healthy
and reasonable.

367
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:43,939
For example, expecting respect, honesty,
safety, and kindness is entirely

368
00:25:43,940 --> 00:25:44,740
appropriate.

369
00:25:45,260 --> 00:25:49,420
However, other expectations can place
unnecessary pressure on a relationship.

370
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:53,160
Let us examine some of the myths
shown on this slide.

371
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:57,180
The first is the belief that a spouse
will always make you happy.

372
00:25:57,980 --> 00:26:01,180
Marriage can bring enormous happiness
and companionship.

373
00:26:01,560 --> 00:26:06,379
However, our spouse cannot be responsible for
every aspect of our emotional

374
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:07,180
wellbeing.

375
00:26:07,700 --> 00:26:12,779
Both individuals need to maintain their own
personal growth, interests, and sources

376
00:26:12,780 --> 00:26:13,580
of support.

377
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:17,800
The second myth is that successful
couples never disagree.

378
00:26:19,220 --> 00:26:24,139
This is simply unrealistic through individuals
will naturally have different

379
00:26:24,140 --> 00:26:25,680
opinions and preferences.

380
00:26:26,360 --> 00:26:31,659
The important issue is not whether disagreements
occur, but whether they are managed

381
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:32,460
respectfully.

382
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:37,780
The third myth is that our spouse should
automatically know what we need.

383
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:42,899
Sometimes people become disappointed because
their partner has not recognized an

384
00:26:42,900 --> 00:26:45,580
expectation that was never communicated.

385
00:26:46,760 --> 00:26:49,720
Healthy relationships require
clear conversations.

386
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:53,680
The fourth myth is that love
alone is enough.

387
00:26:54,130 --> 00:26:59,820
Love is important, but lasting relationships
also require trust, compatibility,

388
00:27:00,540 --> 00:27:03,660
communication, responsibility, and effort.

389
00:27:04,580 --> 00:27:09,340
Another common misconception is that marriage will
solve all our personal problems.

390
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:14,579
Marriage can provide support, but it cannot
automatically resolve emotional

391
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:19,100
difficulties, financial challenges, or
unresolved personal issues.

392
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:24,779
Finally, some individuals enter marriage
expecting their spouse to change

393
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:25,580
completely.

394
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:30,739
Although people can grow and develop, marriage
should not begin with the assumption

395
00:27:30,740 --> 00:27:34,820
that we can transform another person into
someone entirely different.

396
00:27:35,680 --> 00:27:39,580
This is why honest conversations before
marriage are so important.

397
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:45,159
We need to understand not only what we hope for,
also what we are realistically

398
00:27:45,160 --> 00:27:46,640
prepared to contribute.

399
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:53,620
Marriage is not a relationship that remains
exactly the same throughout life.

400
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:54,800
It evolves.

401
00:27:55,160 --> 00:27:59,139
The circumstances of a couple during the first
year of marriage may be very

402
00:27:59,140 --> 00:28:03,200
different from those they experienced
10 or 20 years later.

403
00:28:04,060 --> 00:28:07,899
In the early stages couples may be focused on
getting to know one another more

404
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:08,700
deeply.

405
00:28:09,140 --> 00:28:13,519
They may be establishing routines, adjusting to
shared responsibilities, building

406
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:16,440
emotional connection, and
planning their future.

407
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:21,559
There may be excitement, romance, and the
experience of beginning a new chapter

408
00:28:21,560 --> 00:28:22,360
together.

409
00:28:22,820 --> 00:28:24,680
However, as life progresses,

410
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:26,460
circumstances change.

411
00:28:27,320 --> 00:28:32,259
Careers develop, financial responsibilities may
increase, some couples become

412
00:28:32,260 --> 00:28:38,319
parents, others may experience fertility
challenges, illnesses, bereavement, caring

413
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:41,260
responsibilities, or changes
in employment.

414
00:28:42,480 --> 00:28:46,200
Each stage can bring new
opportunities and new pressures.

415
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:51,040
Consider a couple who initially have
considerable time to spend together.

416
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:58,200
They may enjoy regular outings, shared activities,
and uninterrupted conversations.

417
00:28:59,300 --> 00:29:02,800
Several years later, their circumstances
may be very different.

418
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:05,480
They may have demanding careers.

419
00:29:05,800 --> 00:29:08,680
Young children are elderly parents
who require support.

420
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:12,920
The relationship must adapt to
these new realities.

421
00:29:13,760 --> 00:29:17,439
This does not mean that love or
companionship should disappear.

422
00:29:17,440 --> 00:29:21,559
It means that couples may need to find
different ways of maintaining their

423
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:22,360
connection.

424
00:29:23,140 --> 00:29:26,600
For example, spending meaningful time
together may require more planning.

425
00:29:27,580 --> 00:29:30,180
Household responsibilities may
need to be reviewed.

426
00:29:31,460 --> 00:29:33,620
Communication may need to become
more intentional.

427
00:29:34,560 --> 00:29:38,420
The important message is that marriage
requires lifelong learning.

428
00:29:38,900 --> 00:29:42,599
We should never assume that because we understood
our spouse at the beginning of the

429
00:29:42,600 --> 00:29:46,880
marriage, we will automatically understand
every change they experience.

430
00:29:47,440 --> 00:29:53,180
Successful couples continue to communicate,
listen, learn, and adapt.

431
00:29:53,960 --> 00:29:58,999
The foundations of respect, kindness, and trust
should remain constant, even when

432
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:00,640
circumstances change.

433
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:05,680
Apply what we have learned to situations
that couples may experience.

434
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:08,720
Our first scenario involves family time.

435
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:14,739
Imagine that a couple disagree about how much time
they should spend visiting their

436
00:30:14,740 --> 00:30:15,740
respective families.

437
00:30:16,580 --> 00:30:20,120
One spouse feels that they should visit
their parents every weekend.

438
00:30:20,900 --> 00:30:23,800
The other feels that they need more time
together as a couple.

439
00:30:24,860 --> 00:30:28,660
Both individuals may have understandable
reasons for their preferences.

440
00:30:29,220 --> 00:30:34,079
However, problems can arise if either person
assumes that their own expectation

441
00:30:34,080 --> 00:30:36,460
should automatically take priority.

442
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:42,839
An unhealthy response would be to dismiss the
other person's feelings or make

443
00:30:42,840 --> 00:30:44,520
unnatural decisions.

444
00:30:45,620 --> 00:30:50,099
A healthier response would involve discussing
their needs and exploring a fair

445
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:50,900
arrangement.

446
00:30:52,020 --> 00:30:57,839
They might agree to alternate visits, arrange
different days, or identify a routine

447
00:30:57,840 --> 00:31:01,820
that respects their family commitments whilst
protecting their time together.

448
00:31:02,960 --> 00:31:07,319
The important lesson is that respectful
consultation can help couples manage

449
00:31:07,320 --> 00:31:09,160
competing expectations.

450
00:31:10,840 --> 00:31:13,420
Our second scenario involves loneliness.

451
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:17,779
Imagine someone entering marriage with the
belief that their spouse will

452
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:22,700
automatically eliminate every feeling of
loneliness or emotional difficulty.

453
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:27,700
This expectation places an enormous
responsibility on the other person.

454
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:32,880
A spouse can provide companionship,
affection, and emotional support.

455
00:31:33,320 --> 00:31:37,879
However, they cannot be expected to meet every
emotional need or resolve every

456
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:38,940
personal challenge.

457
00:31:40,040 --> 00:31:43,980
Healthy relationships involve both
connection and individual wellbeing.

458
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:50,199
This means maintaining appropriate relationships
with family and friends, pursuing

459
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:53,840
meaningful interests and seeking
professional support when needed.

460
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:58,920
As you reflect on these scenarios,
consider how you would respond.

461
00:31:59,720 --> 00:32:01,980
Would you be willing to listen to a
different perspective?

462
00:32:02,880 --> 00:32:06,400
Could you acknowledge that your spouse's
needs may differ from your own?

463
00:32:07,200 --> 00:32:11,280
Would you be prepared to compromise without
feeling that you had lost?

464
00:32:12,020 --> 00:32:16,659
These are the kinds of questions that help us
develop the emotional maturity

465
00:32:16,660 --> 00:32:18,260
required for marriage.

466
00:32:19,980 --> 00:32:23,080
We have now reached the final slide
of our first module.

467
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:26,300
I would like to conclude with a very
important message.

468
00:32:26,900 --> 00:32:30,819
Throughout the process of finding the spouse, we
naturally spend time thinking about

469
00:32:30,820 --> 00:32:32,960
the qualities we want in another person.

470
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:38,539
We may think about their faith, personality,
appearance, education, family

471
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:40,020
background, or ambition.

472
00:32:41,940 --> 00:32:44,780
These are all matters that may
influence compatibility.

473
00:32:45,620 --> 00:32:49,520
However, there is another question that
deserves just as much attention.

474
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:53,160
Am I preparing myself to be a good spouse?

475
00:32:54,120 --> 00:32:56,080
This is what we call the mirror check.

476
00:32:57,460 --> 00:33:00,220
I would like you to reflect on
these six questions.

477
00:33:02,360 --> 00:33:06,340
Firstly, am I emotionally ready to share my
life with another person?

478
00:33:07,360 --> 00:33:11,739
A marriage involves considering another person's
needs, feelings, and circumstances

479
00:33:11,740 --> 00:33:13,380
alongside our own.

480
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:19,120
Secondly, can I truly listen to views
that differ from my own?

481
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:22,900
Being a good listener does not mean
agreeing with everything.

482
00:33:23,080 --> 00:33:26,160
It means being willing to understand
another person's perspective.

483
00:33:27,820 --> 00:33:30,400
Thirdly, am I willing to
acknowledge my mistakes?

484
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:35,199
We all make mistakes, but emotional maturity
requires us to take responsibility

485
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:37,580
rather than automatically blaming others.

486
00:33:38,760 --> 00:33:43,400
Fourthly, do I understand the practical
responsibilities involved in marriage?

487
00:33:44,280 --> 00:33:48,860
These may include financial commitments, household
responsibilities, communication,

488
00:33:49,240 --> 00:33:53,780
family responsibilities, and relationships
and making decisions together.

489
00:33:55,040 --> 00:33:59,640
Fifthly, am I prepared to support someone during
their difficult circumstances?

490
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:03,040
Marriage is not simply about
sharing happy moments.

491
00:34:03,160 --> 00:34:07,200
It also involves showing compassion and
support during challenges.

492
00:34:08,020 --> 00:34:08,820
Finally,

493
00:34:09,700 --> 00:34:13,280
can I respect another person's
boundaries and independence?

494
00:34:14,900 --> 00:34:19,120
A healthy marriage requires mutual
respect, dignity, and trust.

495
00:34:19,880 --> 00:34:24,279
I encourage you to spend some time considering
these questions after completing this

496
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:25,080
lesson.

497
00:34:25,440 --> 00:34:27,800
You do not need to have perfect answers.

498
00:34:28,100 --> 00:34:33,339
The purpose of reflection is to identify where you
are now and how you can continue

499
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:34,140
developing.

500
00:34:34,840 --> 00:34:39,539
As we conclude, remember the three foundations we
explored at the beginning of this

501
00:34:39,540 --> 00:34:40,340
module.

502
00:34:40,960 --> 00:34:41,760
Sakina,

503
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:43,840
Bawada, and Rahma.

504
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:47,220
Tranquility, affection, and mercy.

505
00:34:47,880 --> 00:34:52,499
These qualities remind us that marriage should be a
source of peace, compassion, and

506
00:34:52,500 --> 00:34:53,600
meaningful connection.

507
00:34:54,280 --> 00:34:58,759
The successful marriage is not built simply by
finding someone who appears perfect.

508
00:34:58,760 --> 00:35:04,379
It is built when two individuals commit to
treating one another with kindness,

509
00:35:04,380 --> 00:35:05,320
respect,

510
00:35:06,360 --> 00:35:07,420
honesty, and responsibility.

511
00:35:08,560 --> 00:35:13,619
At LifeLink, we want you to enter marriage with
knowledge, confidence, and realistic

512
00:35:13,620 --> 00:35:14,520
expectations.

513
00:35:15,360 --> 00:35:19,520
This is why your learning journey continues
beyond this first module.

514
00:35:20,880 --> 00:35:24,940
In module two, we will explore finding
the right life partner.

515
00:35:25,620 --> 00:35:29,340
We will examine the importance of
character, religious compatibility,

516
00:35:30,060 --> 00:35:35,499
emotional compatibility, family background,
attraction, shared values, and

517
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:37,920
recognizing potential warning signs.

518
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:42,779
Before moving on, please compete the end of
module assessment to check your

519
00:35:42,780 --> 00:35:45,600
understanding of the three
principles we have explored.

520
00:35:46,300 --> 00:35:50,260
Thank you for joining us for module one of
the LifeLink Marriage Academy.

521
00:35:50,860 --> 00:35:55,679
May Allah grant you beneficial knowledge, wisdom
in your decisions, and a marriage

522
00:35:55,680 --> 00:35:59,220
founded upon tranquility,
affection, and mercy.

523
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:00,560
Ameen.

524
00:36:00,800 --> 00:36:01,899
Salaamu 'alaykum.

525
00:36:01,900 --> 00:36:04,000
Warahmatullahi warahmatullahi wabarakatuh.

